def inert_primes(d, bound):
    if d % 4 == 1:
        b, c = -1, (1 - d) // 4      # x^2 - x - (d - 1)/4
    else:
        b, c = 0, -d                 # x^2 - d
    out = []
    for p in range(2, bound):
        if any(p % q == 0 for q in range(2, p)):
            continue
        if all((x * x + b * x + c) % p for x in range(p)):
            out.append(p)
    return out
```

For $d = -1$ the inert primes are those $\equiv 3 \pmod 4$; for $d = 5$ those $\equiv \pm 2 \pmod 5$, including $2$ (because $x^2 + x + 1$ is irreducible mod $2$, not because of $x^2 - 5$); for $d = -5$ those $\equiv 11, 13, 17, 19 \pmod{20}$; for $d = 2$ those $\equiv \pm 3 \pmod 8$. In each case the answer depends only on $p$ modulo $|d_K|$ — quadratic reciprocity at work.
